course: chemistry
lesson: 7
title: Oil and water, and things that won't mix
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Pour oil into a glass of water and it sits on top in its own layer, however long you wait.
explain: Water molecules cling to each other far more than to oil, so they squeeze the oil out, and oil, being lighter for its size, floats.
explain: Shake a bottle of salad dressing and the oil breaks into tiny droplets for a while, but the droplets soon join up again.
explain: Egg yolk can keep the droplets apart for good: its molecules have one end that likes water and one that likes oil, which is how thick, creamy mayonnaise is made.
explain: The same rule explains greasy fingers that water won't clean, ducks that stay dry, and the fat that sets on top of cold soup.
fact: Oil and water do not mix; they form two layers with oil on top, because oil is less dense than water.
fact: Shaking breaks oil into small droplets, but they join up again unless something holds them apart.
fact: Mustard or egg yolk helps oil droplets stay apart longer in a dressing.
fact: Mayonnaise is an emulsion: tiny oil droplets held apart in egg yolk and lemon juice; milk is an emulsion of tiny fat drops in water, and the drops scatter light, which is why milk looks white.
fact: A thin film of oil on a puddle shows rainbow colours, as soap bubbles do, because light reflects off its top and bottom; the colours depend on how thick the film is, so they swirl as it spreads and drifts.
fact: Spilled oil floats on the sea and coats birds' feathers, so they cannot keep warm or fly; rescuers wash them with washing-up liquid.
fact: Grease does not dissolve in water; soap links grease and water so it can be rinsed away, and warm water helps.
fact: In a lava lamp, wax heated at the bottom expands, becomes less dense and rises, then cools and sinks.
fact: Honey, water and oil poured into one glass make three layers, with the densest, honey, at the bottom.
fact: Density means how much stuff is packed into the same space.
fact: Ducks spread oil from a gland near the tail over their feathers, so water runs off; that is why they preen so much.
fact: Fat from cooled soup rises and sets on top; poured down the sink it hardens in pipes and helps build huge blockages called "fatbergs".
exercise: You pour oil into a glass of water. Where does the oil end up? | Floating on top | Sinking to the bottom | Mixed evenly through the water | Half on top and half at the bottom
exercise: Why does oil float on water? | It is less dense, and water pushes it out | It is full of tiny air bubbles, like a sponge or a cork | It is warmer than the water | Water pulls it up towards the air
exercise: Why do you shake salad dressing before pouring it? | To break the oil into droplets mixed with the vinegar for a while | To make the oil dissolve in the vinegar for good, so it never separates again | To warm the dressing up | To get the air out of it
exercise: What keeps mayonnaise thick and creamy instead of separating? | Egg yolk keeps oil drops apart | Lots of salt makes all the oil dissolve | It is kept cold so the oil stays frozen | Tiny air bubbles hold the oil up
exercise: Why are there rainbow colours on a puddle in a car park? | A very thin film of oil reflects light in colours | The puddle is full of coloured paint washed off the cars | The sun shines through coloured rain | Cars leave crayons on the ground
exercise: Why does an oil spill harm seabirds? | Oil sticks to their feathers, so they cannot keep warm or fly | Oil makes the sea far too hot for the birds to dive and catch fish | Oil dissolves their bones | Oil makes the fish taste sweet
exercise: Why won't water alone clean greasy fingers? | Grease does not dissolve in water, you need soap | Water is too thin to touch grease | Grease is a gas that floats away as soon as water touches it | Water makes grease thicker
exercise: Why do the blobs in a lava lamp rise? | Heated wax expands, becomes less dense and floats up | A tiny pump hidden at the bottom pushes them up one at a time | The light bulb pulls them up | The blobs are filled with helium
exercise: Honey, oil and water poured into one glass make three layers. Which is at the bottom? | Honey, the densest | Oil, the slipperiest | Water, the clearest | They all mix together
exercise: Why does a duck stay dry in a pond? | It spreads oil on its feathers so water runs off | Its feathers are made of a kind of plastic | Ducks never really get in the water | The pond water is too cold to soak in
exercise: Fat makes a solid layer on top of cooled soup. Why on top? | Fat is lighter than water and does not mix with it | Fat is heavier than water | The cold air in the fridge pulls the fat upwards | The soup pushes it up when it freezes
exercise: What is density? | How much stuff is packed into the same space | How hot or cold a liquid is | How thick a glass is | How fast a liquid pours from a jug
exercise: Why does the dressing recipe say to add mustard? | It helps the oil droplets stay apart longer | It makes the oil heavier than the vinegar, so it sinks | It turns the oil into water | It stops the bottle from leaking
exercise: Why should cooking fat go in the bin, not down the sink? | It sets in the pipes and helps make big blockages | It makes the water in the pipes far too hot | It turns the sink green | It dissolves the metal of the pipes
test: Your friend says: "If I shake oil and water hard enough they'll stay mixed forever." What actually happens? | They mix for a moment, then separate again | They stay mixed together forever and never come apart | They turn into a solid | They both turn into gas
test: "why's milk white anyway, it's just water innit" Best answer? | It is an emulsion: tiny fat drops in water scatter light | It has white paint added to it at the farm before it is bottled | Cows only drink white water | It is white because it is cold
test: What would happen if you left a lava lamp switched off? | The wax would sit at the bottom | The blobs would keep rising and falling forever | The wax would dissolve in the liquid | The lamp would get hot and explode
test: A cartoon shows rescuers cleaning an oily seabird with plain cold water. What should they use instead? | Washing-up liquid, which lifts the oil off | Vinegar, which dissolves the oil but also the feathers | Nothing, oil falls off by itself | Sand, which scrubs the oil away
test: Why do ducks keep preening with their beaks? | To spread oil over their feathers and keep waterproof | To dry their feathers with their breath | To count their feathers | To pick off and eat the insects that live on the water
test: Your gran pours bacon fat down the sink. What's the risk? | It sets in the pipes and helps build blockages called fatbergs | It cleans the pipes | It turns into water once it reaches the drain under the street | It helps the pipes stay warm
test: In a tower of honey, water and oil, a grape sinks through oil and water and rests on the honey. Why? | The grape is denser than water and oil but less dense than honey | The grape is sticky, and the honey grabs it and holds it on top | The grape is lighter than oil | Grapes always sink to the very bottom
test: "pan's greasy as hell, cold water does nothing" Best fix? | Use warm water and soap | Use more cold water, faster | Scrub it with salt and cold water | Just wipe it with a dry cloth
test: Why do the rainbow colours on a puddle swirl and move? | The oil film keeps changing thickness as it spreads and drifts | The puddle is full of tiny fish swimming round and round under the top | Rain falls in coloured drops | The ground underneath is changing colour
test: Your little sister thinks mayonnaise is "just oil". What else holds it together? | Egg yolk, whose molecules sit around each oil droplet | Glue made from vinegar | Lots of sugar | Frozen water from the fridge, which keeps the oil in place
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the behaviour of oil upon water? | The oil forms a distinct layer upon the surface, being less dense than water | The oil dissolves wholly within the water | The oil descends to the base of the vessel, being of greater density than water | The oil is converted to water upon contact
exam: Identify the principal function of egg yolk in the preparation of mayonnaise. | Its molecules maintain the separation of minute droplets of oil | It renders the oil wholly soluble in water | It solidifies the oil by cooling | It supplies the greater part of the oil of which mayonnaise consists
exam: It is given that honey, water and oil are introduced into a single vessel. Ascertain the liquid which shall occupy the lowest position. | Honey | The water | The oil | None, the liquids mixing uniformly
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the disposal of cooking fat into domestic drainage. | It solidifies within pipes and contributes to severe blockages | It dissolves harmlessly and completely in the water of the drains | It improves the flow of water through pipes | It evaporates entirely before reaching the sewer
